2 FORM jpg
2 TITL María del Carmen Echeverri Echagüe
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE María del Carmen Echeverri Echagüe is my 7th great grandmother. The photo design I composed in her honour has the typical arepas, or flat white corn round sort of cakes you usually have for breakfast in the paisa region of Colombi
3 CONC a. My father, Alberto Ferreira Palacio, taught us how to make them at home, rather than buy them commercially. The tradition passes from one generation to the other. The houses portrayed are typical colonial paisa houses. Finally,
3 CONC  the “Paisa Tray” represented by a plate served with steamed rice, red beans, meat, fried plaintain, fried por rinds, avocado slice, sausages, and fried egg. My grandmother’s name was in honour of the Virgin of Carmen, to whom the
3 CONC  gold miners and people going to travel through hazardous ways and roads usually pray asking for protection. My dad was a fervent believer in the Virgin del Carmen, even stating She had saved him by 2 centimeters of having fall in
3 CONC to a deep abyss. From Rionegro, Antioquia, a branch of María del Carmen Echeverri’s descendants ended up forming part of the Paisa colonization that founded Manizales and Líbano, Tolima, among others. I belong to this family’s bra
3 CONC nch.
